'''White man’s footprint''', a name given by the American Indians to the common plantain, ''Plantago major'', supposed to appear wherever white men settle.—'''White man’s weed.''' See ''whiteweed''.—'''White maple.''' See ''silver maple'', under ''maple''<sup>1</sup>.—'''White meat.''' (''a'') Food made of milk, butter, cheese, eggs, and the like.
{{smaller block|How cleanly he wipes his spoon at every spoonful of any ''whitemeat'' he eats!
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Every Man out of his Humour, iv. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|Look you, sir, the northern man loves ''white-meats'', the southern man sallads.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, i. 3.}}}}
(''b'') Certain delicate flesh used for food, as poultry, rabbits, veal, and pork.
{{smaller block|Fish was enormously consumed, and so, too, were ''white meat'' and dairy produce.
{{right|''H. Hall'', Society in Elizabethan Age, vi.}}}}
(''c'') Same as ''light meat''. See ''meat''<sup>1</sup>.—'''White melilot.''' See ''Melilotus''.—'''White metal, mignonette, money.''' See the nouns.—'''White Moors''', the Genoese. See the quotation.
{{smaller block|It is proverbially said there are in Genoa Mountaines without wood, Sea without fish, Women without shame, and Men without conscience, which makes them to be termed the ''White Moores''.
{{right|''Howell'', Forreine Travell (ed. Arber), p. 41.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
White mouse. (''a'') One of a fancy breed of the common house-mouse, an albino of ''Mus musculus.'' The albinism originates by chance, like that of many other animals, but may be perfected and perpetuated by methodical selection. When it is perfect, the mice are snow-white, with pink eyes, nose, ears, paws, and tail. (''b'') The lemming of Hudson’s Bay, ''Cuniculus torquatus;'' the snow-mouse, which turns pure-white in winter.—'''White mulberry, mullen, mustard.''' See the nouns.—'''White nettle,''' the white dead-nettle, ''Lamium album.''—'''White nickel,''' nickel diarsenide, the mineral rammelsbergite.—'''White night-hawk.''' Same as ''mutton-bird.''—'''White noddy,''' the white tern. See cut under ''Gygis.''—'''White nosegay-tree.''' See ''nosegay-tree.''—'''White note.''' See def. 10 and ''note''<sup>1</sup>.—'''White nun,''' the smew, ''Mergellus albellus.'' See cut under ''smew.''—'''White oak.''' See ''oak'' (with cut).—'''White oakum.''' See ''oakum'', 2.—'''White olive.''' See ''Halleria.''—'''White owl.''' See ''snow-owl.''—'''White pearwood,''' a South African tree, ''Pterocelastrus rostratus,'' of the ''Celastraceæ.'' It has a height of about 20 feet, and yields a heavy, strong, and durable wood, much used for wagon-work.—'''White pepper.''' See ''pepper.''—'''White perch,''' a very common food-fish of eastern North America, ''Morone americana,'' of the family ''Labracidæ.'' It is thus not a true perch, or member of the ''Per-''
