readings.c3s3:
{{smaller|color’s retaining the name of ''white''.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>My Nan shall be the queen of all the fairies,
Finely attired in a robe of ''white''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., iv. 4. 72.}}}}
'''2.''' A pigment of this color.—'''3.''' Something, or a part of something, having the color of snow. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') The central part in the butt in archery, which was formerly painted white; the center or mark at which an arrow or other missile is aimed; hence, the thing or point aimed at.}}
{{smaller block|Vertue is the ''white'' we shoote at, not vanitie.
{{right|''Lyly'', Euphues and his England, p. 245.}}}}
{{smaller block|’Twas I won the wager, though you hit the ''white''.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., v. 2. 186.}}}}
{{smaller block|Thus Geneva Lake swallowed up the Episcopal Sea, and Church-Lands were made secular, which was the ''White'' they levell’d at.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, iii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') The albumen of an egg, or that pellucid viscous fluid which surrounds the yolk; also, sometimes, the corresponding part of a seed, or the farinaceous matter surrounding the embryo. (''c'') That part of the ball of the eye which surrounds the iris or colored part.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And he, poor heart, no sooner heard my news,
But turns me up his ''whites'', and falls flat down.</poem>
{{right|''Grim the Collier'', iii. (''Davies.'')}}}}
{{smaller block|Ay, and I turned up the ''whites'' of my eyen till the strings awmost cracked again.
{{right|''Macklin'', Man of the World, iii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller|(''d'') ''pl.'' In ''printing'', blank spaces. (''e'') ''pl.'' A white fabric otherwise called ''long cloth.''}}
{{smaller block|The Indians doe bring fine ''whites'', which the Tartars do all roll about their heads, & al other kinds of ''whites'', which serue for apparell.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 332.}}}}
{{smaller block|Salisbury has . . . Long Cloths for the Turkey trade, called Salisbury ''Whites''.
{{right|''Defoe'', Tour thro’ Great Britain, I. 324. (''Davies.'')}}}}

readings.c3s4:
¶
(''f''†) White clothing or drapery.

{{smaller block|You clothe Christ with your blacks on earth, he will clothe you with his glorious ''whites'' in heaven.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, II. 174.}}}}

(''g'') A member of the white race of mankind: as, the “poor ''whites''” of the southern United States.

'''4.''' ''pl.'' In ''med.'', leucorrhea.—'''Body white.''' {{smaller|See ''flake-white''.}}—'''China white,''' {{smaller|a very pure variety of white lead, usually in small drops. Also ''silver-white''.}}—'''Chinese white.''' {{smaller|Same as ''zinc white''.}}—'''Clichy white,''' {{smaller|a kind of white lead made at Clichy, in France.}}—'''Constant white,''' {{smaller|an artificially prepared sulphate of barium. See ''blanc fixe'', under ''blanc''.}}—'''Cremnitz white.''' {{smaller|See ''Kremnitz white''.}}—'''Dutch white,''' {{smaller|an adulterated white lead: a book-name.}}—'''Faenza white,''' {{smaller|a name given to the fine white enamel of some varieties of majolica. It is thought, however, that the discovery is due to the factory of Ferrara.}}—'''Flake white.''' {{smaller|See ''flake-white''.}}—'''Forest whites†.''' {{smaller|Same as ''penistone''.}}—'''French white,''' {{smaller|a variety of white lead: same as ''China white''. Also called ''blanc d’argent''.}}—'''In black and white.''' {{smaller|See ''black''.}}—'''Indophenol white.''' {{smaller|Same as ''leuco-''}}
